A||||9||FieroBert||V-6 Oil Leak||||5||
Z||000000||FieroBert||05-21-2001||08:03 PM||||I have a severe leak on my 86 GT.  I've cleaned things off as best I can and it would appear to be leaking from the front of the engine (Passenger side of car).  I cannot locate the leak, as it seems to coat the entire frame rail and drips from there.  Oil pan gasket looks good, can't see well enough to determine the front valve cover.  Harmonic balancer and pulley look dry but oil is coating the water pump and even higher.  Wondering if it is the front main seal and just getting slung around by the pulley.  Any suggestions?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Wade87GT||05-21-2001||09:48 PM||||Usually the first place for a 2.8 oil leak is from the distributor shaft o-ring. I know it's on the other end, but depending how your engine is sitting, it may be drooling down through the intake valley. Look at the base of the shaft and if you see fresh/wet oil, that's probably it. Or, it might be a valve cover gasket.<P>Wade||||reg||3
Z||000002||DJRice||05-21-2001||10:35 PM||||My oil pressure sender switch sprung a leak and caused a big mess on that side of the car a couple of years ago....Its a small metal can between your battery and the EGR solenoid assembly.  <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djr_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT GT 87||05-21-2001||10:48 PM||||fROM YOUR DESCRIPTION IT HAS TO BE THE OIL PRESS. SENDER . VALVE COVERS DON'T LEAK THAT WAY. i HAD THE SAME LEAK FROM THAT SENDER. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroBert||05-21-2001||11:22 PM||||Thanks - its getting late so I wiped the sensor and fitting down as best I could.  Will recheck it tomorrow.  Usually leaves a small puddle after driving it, so it should show up real soon.  Seemed as though there may have been fresh oil at the fitting on the bottom.  Does the sensor go bad or can it just need tightened up.  Can't find metion of it in my Haynes manual, how does on go about removing it?||||reg||1
Z||000005||LivoniaFieroGT||05-22-2001||09:24 AM||||It takes a 9/16th wrench to remove this part (o/p sensor).  It can be done in little increments with the battery in its tray.  However, for the sake of speed, loosen battery and shield at tray and move out of the way.  New part must have the thread sealer on it.  GM originals should be available at any dealer.  If not I have a couple.  Don't over torque <BR>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroBert||05-22-2001||06:26 PM||||Well it would appear that this is where my oil is escaping from - thanks for the correct diagnosis.<P>Went to move the EGR solenoid vacuum line out of the way and found it to be about 18 inches long with a screw plugging it up.  So I have another project - the list is growing daily.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Devotshka||05-24-2001||01:53 AM||||I fought a similar oil leak for a LONG time, in the end it was the front timing cover/oil pan seal (the tiny cork gasket about 6 inches long).  That gasket is only on 85-86 V6s, apparently 87-88 have that part integrated into main oil pan gasket.||||reg||3
Z||000008||FieroBert||05-28-2001||10:53 AM||||Just a follow up to close this topic - I now have a dry garage floor for the first time since ownership of my Fiero.  It was the oil pressure sender, but the unit itself was OK.  Put new sealant on the threads and retightened the sensor, cleaned everything down and no new oil has shown up.  Thanks for the accurate diagnosis.<P>Of course everytime I work on the car I end up with a longer list.  The battery hold-down bolt broke and will require probably more effort than the fixing of the oil leak.||||reg||1
Z||000009||StuGood||05-28-2001||01:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroBert:<BR><B>...Went to move the EGR solenoid vacuum line out of the way and found it to be about 18 inches long with a screw plugging it up...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If the vacuum line is the one by itself, extending from the forward end of the EGR solenoid, that one is the vent line.  It's supposed to be connected to a metal line on the firewall forward of the engine, near the A/C compressor - it goes to the "clean" side of the air filter.     <P>||||reg||1
